2 regular expressions I used:
1. "^\\s*(final\\s+)?(int|double|String|boolean|char)\\s+(.+);\\s*$"
    - Variable declaration pattern.
    This regex matches a variable declaration line in Sjava.
    It allows optional whitespaces and an optional final keyword,
    then requires one of the valid types (int, double, String, boolean, or char).
    After the type, it matches everything, which includes the variable name or
    names and possible initialization, and ensures the line ends with a semicolon.
    The start (^) and end ($) anchors make sure the whole line follows this structure.
2. "^\\s*void\\s+([a-zA-Z]\\w*)\\s*\\(([^)]*)\\)\\s*\\{\\s*$"
    - Method declaration pattern.
    This regular expression matches a method declaration line. It allows optional whitespaces,
    requires the void keyword at the start, and then matches a valid method name
    that starts with a letter.
    It captures the parameter list inside parentheses (which can be empty) and
    requires an opening { at the end of the line. The anchors ensure that only a full,
    correctly formatted method declaration is accepted.

Design choices and responsibility division:
The main design choice was to separate syntax recognition from semantic validation
and to keep each module responsible for one clear aspect of the language.
Regular expressions are used only to recognize high-level structures
(such as “this line looks like a variable declaration” or “this is a method header”),
while deeper correctness checks (types, scopes, initialization, legality of assignments)
are handled by dedicated validator and scope classes.
This keeps the parser readable, avoids overly complex regexes,
and makes the logic easier to debug and extend.
Modules:
- main: Responsible for program entry and I/O handling.
- entities: Represent the language elements.
These classes store structured information about variables, methods, values, and types,
without containing validation logic.
- scope: Manages variable visibility and lookup. It models nested scopes (global, method, if/while blocks).
- validation: Each validator is responsible for validating one specific language construct.
- parser: Coordinates the whole process.
It reads the file, performs a two-pass parse (first collecting method signatures, then validating code),
manages scopes and block structure, and delegates specific checks to validators.